    description: "Liturgy refers to the formal, structured pattern of public worship followed by a particular religious community, encompassing the specific prescribed order of prayers, scripture readings, hymns, and ritual actions that together constitute a community's own regular, recognized official form of communal religious worship. Liturgical traditions, including Roman Catholicism, Eastern Orthodoxy, and certain Protestant denominations, including Anglicanism and Lutheranism, follow especially formalized, carefully prescribed liturgies, often set out in detail in an official prayer book or missal, and generally organized around a recurring structured annual liturgical calendar that marks significant recurring religious seasons and important commemorative feast days throughout the year. Other Christian traditions, particularly many evangelical and nondenominational Protestant churches, instead favor a considerably less formally structured, more spontaneous approach to communal worship, though even these considerably less liturgically formal traditions typically still follow some genuinely recognizable, broadly consistent recurring pattern of worship from one worship service to the next. The word liturgy derives from a Greek term originally referring to a public work or service performed on behalf of the broader community, reflecting the underlying traditional theological understanding of formal liturgical worship as a genuinely significant shared corporate act rather than merely a purely private individual devotional exercise. Formal liturgy also holds significant established importance well beyond Christianity, with religions including Judaism and Islam each maintaining their own distinctive prescribed patterns of formal structured communal prayer, reflecting a widely shared broader recognition across many world religious traditions that structured communal worship serves an important spiritual function distinct from purely private individual devotion alone."
